Step-1: Downloading the source code from the CVS repository.
This part is only for those who have an account on max.ecn.purdue.edu for having an access to the CVS repository. (For an account please contact gekco@purdue.edu OR, ssahmed@purdue.edu)

(a) In your command propmt (assumed $) type
    $ cvs co NEMO_3D
    After you This will download few license files along with a script file CVS_nemo3d_setup.sh. 
(b) cd to NEMO_3D folder and run this script file:
    $ cd NEMO_3D
    $ ./CVS_nemo3d_setup.sh
    This command will download all the folders and files from the CVS repository to your local folder.


Step-2: Loading modules 

(a) In your command prompt type
    (on Hamlet)
    $ module load mpich/intel-90032
    (on Lear)
    $ module load mpich64-1.2.7p1-intel/9.1.045
 
Step-3: Building/Making
    cd to build directory in NEMO_3D
    $ cd build/
    Set an environment variable for the XERCESCROOT in NEMO_3D 
    $ setenv XERCESCROOT `pwd`/../xerces-c-src2_1_0
    (You would also find this path at the very end of your downloading all the files n step-1/b.) 
    for example, setenv XERCESCROOT /home/rcac/ssahmed/NEMO_3D/xerces-c-src2_1_0
    $ vi make.inc
    go to the list of compiler options and use (on Hamlet) BUILD_TYPE = intel9_32_mpi_intelfast  and (on Lear) BUILD_TYPE = intel9_64_mpi_intelfast 
    Make sure you comment out all other options. Then type,
    $ make
    (This will take some time, about 15 minutes, and create the executable in nemo3d/bin directory)
 
Step-4: Running

    cd to nemo3d/bin directory.
    $ cd nemo3d/bin
    You would see that an executable (nemo3d.ex) has been created in the bin directory.
    
    (a) For serial job
    Now to run a simple serial job do the following. Go to the example folder and copy one of the example files to the bin folder. Then give the run command, for example

    $ ./nemo3d.ex Sibulk.xml
    
    Outputs will be created in binary format. To convert to ASCII format do
    $ ln -s nemo3d-i386-(press tab key for full name)  fmtdat.ex 
    $ ./fmtdat.ex -a2 SiBulk_nd_Ek
   
    (b) parallel job
    To tun a parallel MPI job you should use the pbs system. Here, how we do it on Hamlet at Purdue:
    $ cd test
    $ ./submit_things.sh pbstemplate.pbs 4 SiBulk_nd_Ek
    You can choose different No. of prcessors other than 4.
    Examples of submit_things.sh and pbstemplate.pbs files are given below.

------------------
submit_things.sh
------------------

#!/bin/sh

# Submits all the named files as nemo3d PBS jobs

if [ x${1}x == "-h" ]; then
	cat <<EOF
Usage: submit_things.sh <template> <#nodes> <input1> <input2> ... <inputN>
EOF
	exit 0
fi

template=$1
shift
nodes=$1
shift
files=$1

for file in $files; do
	echo RUNDIR=run.d
	echo "creating directory for the run"
	echo if [ ! -d $RUNDIR ] then
	echo	mkdir $RUNDIR
	echo fi
	mkdir -p run.d/$nodes.d
        cp $file run.d/$nodes.d/
	echo "Submitting $file"
	cat $template | sed -e "s/MY_NODES/$nodes/g" | sed -e "s/MY_INPUT_FILE/$file/g" > ./run.d/$nodes.d/run.$nodes.pbs
	# Remove the word 'echo' to make this go
	cd run.d/$nodes.d
	qsub run.$nodes.pbs
	cd ../..

done

